            <div xml:id="d1143">

                <pb facs="1164" n="1154" sameAs="frus:frus1949v09/pg1154"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.115/10–2249: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Shanghai</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">McConaughy</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Shanghai</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1949-10-22T11:00:00+08:00">October 22,
                            1949—11 a. m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received October 22—2:43 a. m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>4473. Suggestion made in ConGentel 4294 of October 12 that Department might wish
                    to take action to avoid incident in connection with <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Flying Trader</hi> (reDeptel 2135, October 20, 7 p. m.) was entirely
                    ConGen’s idea. Neither Pattison, Isbrandtsen agent, or skipper <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Flying Trader</hi> had any knowledge this message before
                    or after it was sent.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">McConaughy</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
